---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import FatIntakeCalculator from '@components/islands/FatIntakeCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';
const calc = getCalculator('health', 'fat-intake-calculator')!;
const faqs = [
  { question: 'How much fat should I eat per day?', answer: 'Health authorities recommend that 20–35% of your daily calories come from fat. On a 2,000-calorie diet, that is roughly 44–78 grams of fat per day, since each gram of fat provides 9 calories.' },
  { question: 'How do I calculate my fat intake in grams?', answer: 'Multiply your daily calories by the fat percentage, then divide by 9 (the calories in a gram of fat). For 2,000 calories at 30%: 2,000 × 0.30 = 600 calories from fat, ÷ 9 ≈ 67 grams a day.' },
  { question: 'Is dietary fat bad for you?', answer: 'No — fat is essential for hormones, vitamin absorption and cell health. What matters is the type: favour unsaturated fats and limit saturated and trans fats.' },
  { question: 'What is the difference between healthy and unhealthy fats?', answer: 'Unsaturated fats (olive and avocado oil, nuts, seeds, oily fish) support heart health and should make up most of your intake. Saturated fats (fatty meat, butter, full-fat dairy) are best kept moderate, and artificial trans fats are best avoided entirely.' },
  { question: 'How much saturated fat should I eat?', answer: 'The Dietary Guidelines cap saturated fat at under 10% of daily calories. The American Heart Association recommends a tighter limit of under 6% for people working to lower their LDL cholesterol. The calculator shows both, as ceilings rather than targets — they are limits not to cross, not amounts to reach.' },
  { question: 'Why does a fat calculator ask for my height and activity?', answer: 'Because every figure it reports is a share of the Calories you burn, not a function of your body weight. It estimates your basal metabolic rate from your age, gender, height and weight, multiplies by your activity level, and takes the recommended shares of the result. That is also why it and the calorie calculator always agree on the calorie figure — it is the same calculation.' },
];
---
<CalculatorLayout
  calculator={calc}
  presentation="task-first"
  intro="Find your recommended daily fat intake in grams — the total-fat range, and the two published limits on saturated fat — from the Calories your own body burns."
  faqs={faqs}
  reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-08-28' }}
>
  <FatIntakeCalculator />
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Choose <strong>US Units</strong> or <strong>Metric Units</strong>. Switching converts figures you have already typed rather than clearing them.</li>
      <li>Enter your <strong>age</strong> and select your <strong>gender</strong>.</li>
      <li>Enter your <strong>height</strong> and <strong>weight</strong>.</li>
      <li>Pick the <strong>activity</strong> level that best matches a typical week.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong> to see your total-fat range and the two limits on saturated fat.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      <strong>+ Settings</strong> holds two choices most people never change: which of the three
      published BMR equations to use for the calorie figure, and a body fat percentage for the one
      that needs it. <strong>Other Units</strong> opens a converter above the calculator for anyone
      whose scales read in stones; it is not a third unit system, and the calculator stays on
      whichever system you chose.
    </p>
    <p>
      You no longer need to bring a calorie figure with you — the calculator works it out from your
      own details, using the same engine as the
      <a href="/health/calorie-calculator">calorie calculator</a>, so the two always agree.
    </p>

    <h2>Why it asks for your body, not your calories</h2>
    <p>
      Every number this calculator reports is a share of the energy you actually use, so it has to
      know what that is. It estimates your basal metabolic rate from your age, gender, height and
      weight, multiplies by your activity level to get your daily Calories, and takes the
      recommended shares of that. Enter the same details here and in the calorie calculator and
      you will get the same calorie figure, because it is the same calculation.
    </p>

    <h2>How much fat you need</h2>
    <p>
      National nutrition guidelines set the Acceptable Macronutrient Distribution Range (AMDR) for
      fat at <strong>20–35% of daily calories</strong>. Fat is not optional: it carries the
      fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K, supplies essential fatty acids, and supports hormone
      production. The range leaves room to shift fat up or down depending on how you allocate
      carbohydrate and protein.
    </p>

    <h2>The formula</h2>
    <p>Because each gram of fat supplies <strong>9 calories</strong>, converting a percentage of calories into grams is straightforward:</p>
    <p><strong>Fat (g) = calories × percentage ÷ 9</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>calories</strong> — your daily calorie target</li>
      <li><strong>percentage</strong> — the share of calories from fat (20% to 35%)</li>
      <li><strong>9</strong> — calories per gram of fat</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The calculator reports the low end (20%) and the high end (35%) as a band, then applies the
      same arithmetic to the two published caps on saturated fat: under 10% of Calories in the
      Dietary Guidelines, and the tighter under 6% the American Heart Association recommends for
      people working to lower their cholesterol. Trans fat gets no row at all, because there is no
      recommended level of it — the guidance is simply to keep it as low as you can.
    </p>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>
      On a <strong>2,000-calorie</strong> day, the low end is 2,000 × 20% ÷ 9 ≈ <strong>44 g</strong>,
      the midpoint is 2,000 × 27.5% ÷ 9 ≈ <strong>61 g</strong>, and the high end is
      2,000 × 35% ÷ 9 ≈ <strong>78 g</strong>. So a fat intake anywhere from about
      <strong>44 to 78 grams a day</strong> fits the guideline at this calorie level.
    </p>

    <h2>Where to sit in the range</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Lower end (~20%)</strong> — leaves more calories for carbohydrate, which suits higher-volume or endurance eating.</li>
      <li><strong>Middle (~27.5%)</strong> — a balanced default for most people.</li>
      <li><strong>Higher end (~35%)</strong> — pairs with lower-carbohydrate patterns and can aid satiety.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Quality matters as much as quantity</h2>
    <p>
      Within your range, favour <strong>unsaturated fats</strong> — olive and avocado oil, nuts,
      seeds and oily fish — which support heart health. Keep <strong>saturated fat</strong> (fatty
      meat, butter, full-fat dairy) moderate, and avoid artificial <strong>trans fats</strong>. The
      gram target is the same whatever the source, but the source is what shapes the health outcome.
    </p>
    <p>
      Pair this with your <a href="/health/protein-calculator">protein target</a> to round out a
      macronutrient plan. This is educational information, not medical or dietary advice.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
